From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: steamac <steamac@local>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 17:14:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] virtio-snd: keep CoreAudio playback control requests and the
 pump from stalling each other

Symptom: multi-second game freezes; the guest driver logs `control message (0x..)
timeout` (PCM_PREPARE/RELEASE) and then `SID 0: invalid I/O queue state` in a storm
(4905 lines, 180 s without frames in one run). The guest counts a playback message as
in flight until the device completes it and refuses the next PREPARE while any is, and
a control message that times out is never retired, so nothing recovers.

Upstream state (libkrun v1.19.6): the CoreAudio backend is steamac's own (patch 0014),
there is nothing upstream to configure. Patch 0024 already moves every capture device
call to its bounded capture worker and gives each stream its own lock; this patch does
the same for playback, on top of it.

Cause, for playback:

* prepare/start/release ran stop, flush, configure (AudioUnitInitialize) and
  AudioOutputUnitStart while holding the stream, which the pump thread takes every
  iteration; the pump could not complete queued I/O meanwhile. The virtio-snd device has
  a single worker thread for every queue, so a control request that blocked in an
  AudioUnit call (or on the stream lock) also stopped it popping the TX queue.
* release() polled the playback drain up to MAX_BUFFER_MS+100 ms with the stream held.
* nothing completed messages when the pump stopped making progress, and nothing bounded
  a control request.

Changes (all in the CoreAudio backend):

* The playback unit lives in a `UnitSlot`; prepare/start/release take it out of the
  stream and run their AudioUnit calls on a short-lived thread (`unit_job`) without the
  stream lock. The pump sees an unconfigured stream meanwhile and carries on. The caller
  waits at most CTL_DEADLINE and otherwise answers IO_ERR instead of never; the job
  finishes in the background and a request meanwhile fails at once as busy. Control
  requests take the stream lock with a deadline too (`lock_host`). Capture keeps the
  unit in its slot as well, configured on 0024's capture worker.
* CTL_DEADLINE is 8 s: a rescue for a call that never returns, not a latency target
  (under memory/CPU pressure a healthy HAL start takes seconds; with a 2 s deadline,
  lean runs A_N19_DX20 / A_N19_VK20 failed PREPAREs and stormed); still below the
  launcher's virtio_snd.msg_timeout_ms of 10 s.
* PREPARE/START/RELEASE that fail (including the deadline) complete the stream's queued
  I/O with IO_ERR (Inner::fail_stream_io), so the guest's next PREPARE is accepted.
* RELEASE waits for the drain (bounded by the ring's consumed pointer) after the stream
  lock is released.
* A watchdog thread fails a stream's queued playback/capture messages with IO_ERR once
  the pump has not reached that stream for IO_STALL (3 s; an idle pump wakes every
  second), so the guest's queues drain and its driver restarts the stream. In-flight
  playback messages moved into `Shared` so the watchdog can reach them without the lock.
* A unit request slower than 250 ms logs where the time went (unit calls, waiting for the
  pump's lock, latency refresh).

Output routing (patch 0017) and the playback tap (patch 0023): the unit slot carries the
OutputRoute and the speaker mixer, so a control request builds the device-bound HAL unit
and its channel map off the stream like any other unit; the pump re-routes through the
same slot and the tap reads the slot's format.

Tests (cargo test --features gpu,snd --lib -- virtio::snd, now run by build.sh): the pump
completes I/O while a unit job is in flight; a stuck unit job fails in time, the next
fails at once as busy, and the stream recovers; control requests fail within the deadline
while the pump holds the stream; a failed PREPARE releases the queued I/O; the deadline
leaves room for a loaded HAL; the watchdog fails queued I/O while the pump is stuck and
leaves an idle pump alone. real_device_cycle_timing is an ignored manual timing of the
real device. The crate's other snd test modules (stream.rs, audio_backends.rs) were
written for the vhost-user-device this code came from and do not compile in libkrun (no
init_logger, NullBackend, MockSplitQueue); they are disabled so the macOS backend's tests
can run.

Limit: AudioUnit calls the pump thread makes itself (output-device switch, resume after
pause, latency refresh) still run under the stream lock; a hang there is covered by the
watchdog and the control deadlines, not prevented.

[steamac sync 1.9.14: folds fork patches 0018 (playback drain), 0022 and 0023 onto
upstream 0024. Their capture start worker (StartGate, stop_pending; fork 0018/0020/0022)
is dropped: 0024's CaptureWorker replaces it.]

